The field trial testing config generator is handed current_os verbatim, so on
this port it receives "haiku" and stops:

    fieldtrial_to_struct.py: error: option --platform: invalid choice: 'haiku'
    (choose from 'android', ..., 'linux', 'mac', 'windows')

The fix belongs in the GN rule, not in the script's platform list. That script
converts the name into a Study::PLATFORM_<NAME> constant from
components/variations/proto/study.proto; teaching it "haiku" would emit a
reference to PLATFORM_HAIKU, which does not exist in the proto, and move the
failure from the generator to the compile. The rule already has precedent for
this kind of mapping -- it rewrites "win" to "windows" two lines above.

Linux is the right name to map to. These are field trial *testing*
configurations, and a trial gated on the Linux platform applies to this port
for the same reasons it applies to Linux.

--- a/chromium/components/variations/field_trial_config/BUILD.gn
+++ b/chromium/components/variations/field_trial_config/BUILD.gn
@@ -25,6 +25,15 @@
 
   if (current_os == "win") {
     platform = "windows"
+  } else if (current_os == "haiku") {
+    # fieldtrial_to_struct.py turns this into a Study::PLATFORM_<NAME> constant
+    # from components/variations/proto/study.proto, so it can only be given a
+    # name that enum already has -- adding "haiku" to the script's list would
+    # generate a reference to PLATFORM_HAIKU and break the compile instead of
+    # the generator. Linux is the honest choice here: these are field trial
+    # test configurations, and every trial gated on the Linux platform applies
+    # to this port for the same reasons.
+    platform = "linux"
   } else {
     platform = current_os
   }
